---
import LegalDocument from '@/components/legal/legal-document.astro';
import PageIntro from '@/components/page-intro.astro';
import SubpageLayout from '@/layouts/subpage-layout.astro';

const toc = [
  { id: 'what-we-collect', title: 'What we collect' },
  { id: 'how-we-use-it', title: 'How we use it' },
  { id: 'ai', title: 'AI and your ChatGPT account' },
  { id: 'sharing', title: 'How sharing works' },
  { id: 'services', title: 'Services that process data' },
  { id: 'storage', title: 'Storage and deletion' },
  { id: 'cookies', title: 'Cookies and device storage' },
  { id: 'payments', title: 'Payments' },
  { id: 'your-rights', title: 'Your rights' },
  { id: 'changes', title: 'Changes to this page' }
];
---

<SubpageLayout
  title="Privacy"
  description="What Groam collects when you plan a trip, why, and the choices you have."
>
  <PageIntro
    title="Privacy"
    lede="What Groam collects when you plan a trip with us, why we need it, and the choices you have."
  />
  <LegalDocument
    draftNote="This is not a complete privacy notice yet. The operator details, legal bases, service-provider list, transfer information, retention periods, and privacy contact still need to be added before launch."
    toc={toc}
    updated="18 October 2026"
  >
    <section id="what-we-collect">
      <h2>What we collect</h2>
      <p>
        Groam stores information you provide and technical records needed to operate your account,
        groups, and trips.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <b>Your account and security.</b> Your name, email address, username, password record,
          optional passkey and two-step-verification records, active sessions, and security details
          such as session expiry, IP address, and browser or device information when available.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Your groups and invitations.</b> The groups you create or join, membership and role
          information, and invitations including the invitee’s email address and their status.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Your trips and activity.</b> Destinations, dates, budgets, itineraries, traveler
          responses, ideas, issues, polls, and conversations, including authorship and change
          history.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Files you add.</b> Covers, photos, and conversation attachments, together with file
          names and types.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>AI activity.</b> Your ChatGPT connection, the models available to it, prompts,
          selected trip context, tool activity, and answers or changes produced in Groam.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="how-we-use-it">
      <h2>How we use it</h2>
      <p>
        We use this information to create and secure accounts; operate groups, invitations, and
        shared trips; keep an authorship and change record; provide conversations and AI features;
        store files you choose to add; and diagnose abuse or service problems. This draft does not
        yet state the legal basis for each purpose.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="ai">
      <h2>AI and your ChatGPT account</h2>
      <p>
        AI is optional. If you connect ChatGPT, Groam stores connection credentials so it can use
        your ChatGPT access when you ask an agent to work. Your prompt, selected parts of the trip,
        and tool requests may be sent to OpenAI. Agents may also search the public web when a task
        calls for it, so search queries and returned pages can involve other websites.
      </p>
      <p>
        Groam asks OpenAI not to store Responses API requests on OpenAI’s side. Groam still stores
        the conversation, agent run, answer, and resulting trip activity needed to show the work in
        the product. OpenAI’s own terms and privacy policy apply to data it receives through your
        connected account. You can disconnect ChatGPT; group planning does not require AI.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="sharing">
      <h2>How sharing works</h2>
      <p>
        A trip belongs to a group. Signed-in members of that group can access the group’s trips and
        shared material, even when a particular traveler has replied “not going” to a trip. Group
        members can see authorship and activity associated with the shared plan. Some assistant
        conversations are private to the person who opens them; trip conversations are shared with
        their participants.
      </p>
      <p>
        Invitation emails disclose the invitee’s address to the service so the invitation can be
        created. Anyone you invite may gain access after joining the group, so only add information
        you are comfortable sharing with the group. Access controls reduce who can open a trip;
        they cannot stop a member from copying or sharing what they can see.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="services">
      <h2>Services that process data</h2>
      <p>
        Groam relies on hosting, database, authentication, file-storage, and operational services
        to run the product. OpenAI receives data when you use connected AI features, as described
        above. The final notice must name the service providers or categories of recipients and
        explain any processing outside your country before this draft is published as final.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="storage">
      <h2>Storage and deletion</h2>
      <p>
        Groam stores account and trip information while it is needed to provide the product. A trip
        can currently be archived, which makes it read-only; archiving is not deletion. Some files
        are removed when their trip item is removed, and ChatGPT connection records are removed
        when the associated user is deleted. The product does not yet publish complete account
        deletion controls or retention periods, so this draft cannot promise a deletion timetable.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="cookies">
      <h2>Cookies and device storage</h2>
      <p>
        The app uses sign-in and session cookies needed to authenticate you and keep the correct
        group open. The public landing site does not currently include advertising or optional
        analytics trackers. If optional tracking is added, this section and any consent controls
        must be updated before that tracking starts.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="payments">
      <h2>Payments</h2>
      <p>
        Planning on your own is free. Inviting travelers will cost €1 per invited traveler,
        for each trip they join, paid by the traveler or covered by the person who invites them. Paid invites are not live yet, so Groam is not collecting payment details
        today. Groam therefore does not collect card or payment-account details today. The privacy
        notice and payment terms must be completed before paid invitations are enabled.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="your-rights">
      <h2>Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        Depending on where you live, privacy law may give you rights over your personal data, such
        as access, correction, deletion, restriction, portability, objection, or a right to complain
        to a data-protection authority. The final notice must explain which rights apply, how to
        exercise them, and where to send a request. No privacy contact is published in this draft
        because the operator has not supplied one.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="changes">
      <h2>Changes to this page</h2>
      <p>
        The date at the top will change when this draft changes. Before a final notice is changed,
        Groam must decide how people will be told about material changes before those changes take
        effect when notice is required.
      </p>
    </section>
  </LegalDocument>
</SubpageLayout>
